import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Local hooks"
  eyebrow="Capabilities"
  description="Run project-local Codex-compatible hooks around Roder sessions, prompts, tools, compaction, and subagents."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/tools/", title: "Tools & policy" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/codex-parity-tools/", title: "Codex-parity tools" }}
>
  <p>
    Roder can load project-local hook configuration from the active workspace and run
    handlers around the same runtime events that drive tools, policy, session state, and
    subagents. A project-specific <code>.roder/hooks.json</code> file takes precedence over
    a copied <code>.codex/hooks.json</code> file, so teams can keep Codex-compatible hook
    files while giving Roder its own workspace policy when needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Hook file lookup</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>.roder/hooks.json</code> is used first when present in the workspace root.</li>
    <li><code>.codex/hooks.json</code> is used as a compatibility fallback.</li>
    <li>Hook files are parsed on demand and cached until their modified time or length changes.</li>
    <li>Invalid matcher regexes are recorded as hook diagnostics instead of silently disabling a hook.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  <p>
    Hook groups live under a top-level <code>hooks</code> object. Roder recognizes{" "}
    <code>PreToolUse</code>, <code>PermissionRequest</code>, <code>PostToolUse</code>,
    <code>PreCompact</code>, <code>PostCompact</code>, <code>SessionStart</code>,
    <code>SessionEnd</code>, <code>UserPromptSubmit</code>, <code>SubagentStart</code>,
    <code>SubagentStop</code>, and <code>Stop</code>. A group can omit{" "}
    <code>matcher</code> to run for every event instance, or provide a regex that is
    matched against the tool name, session startup/resume marker, or subagent type.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>SessionStart</code> runs once for the thread session that opens the runtime.
    Fresh threads use the <code>startup</code> matcher, resumed threads use{" "}
    <code>resume</code>, and repeated thread-store reads do not re-run the setup hook.
    This keeps environment setup, logging, and workspace checks from firing again after
    a turn is already underway or stopped.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "shell|write_file",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "node .roder/hooks/check-tool.js",
            "timeout": 5,
            "statusMessage": "checking workspace policy"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "matcher": "startup|resume",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "scripts/agent-session-start"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Handlers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>command</code> handlers run through the workspace shell, receive the hook payload on stdin, and record stdout on success or stderr on failure.</li>
    <li><code>commandWindows</code> can override the command on Windows, while <code>timeout</code>, <code>statusMessage</code>, <code>async</code>, and <code>additionalContextLimit</code> match the copied Codex field names.</li>
    <li><code>mcp_tool</code> handlers call an installed MCP tool by <code>server</code> and <code>tool</code>, with an optional JSON <code>input</code> object.</li>
    <li><code>prompt</code> and <code>agent</code> handlers are accepted in unit-style Codex files. Roder records them as skipped diagnostics; it does not spawn model work from those handler types.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Command and MCP inputs can interpolate <code>{"${tool_name}"}</code> and{" "}
    <code>{"${tool_input}"}</code>. Recorded hook output is capped globally at 8 KiB, and a
    smaller <code>additionalContextLimit</code> can reduce how much command output is kept.
  </p>

  <h2>PreToolUse decisions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>PreToolUse</code> is the blocking hook point. Hook failures are diagnostics and
    fail open, but an explicit deny blocks the tool call and records an errored tool result
    in the transcript. A successful hook can also return updated tool input, and chained
    hooks see the previous hook's rewrite rather than the model's original arguments.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`{
  "hookSpecificOutput": {
    "permissionDecision": "deny",
    "permissionDecisionReason": "shell commands must go through the repo script"
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <pre><code>{`{
  "hookSpecificOutput": {
    "permissionDecision": "allow",
    "updatedInput": {
      "command": "npm test -- --runInBand"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Payload shape</h2>
  <p>
    Every hook receives a JSON payload with <code>hookEventName</code>,
    <code>sessionId</code>, <code>turnId</code>, <code>cwd</code>,
    <code>workspaceRoot</code>, <code>toolName</code>, <code>toolUseId</code>, and{" "}
    <code>toolInput</code>. Session-scoped hooks use a synthetic{" "}
    <code>turnId</code> of <code>session</code>; turn and tool hooks carry the active
    turn id. For <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>toolInput.source</code> is either{" "}
    <code>startup</code> or <code>resume</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Runtime boundary.</strong> Local hooks complement Roder policy; they do not
    replace it. Tool calls still pass through the configured policy mode, workspace runner,
    transcript persistence, and event stream.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
